Change: Require a trusted proxy for `--proxy-auth-username`

With `--proxy-auth-username`, rest-server accepted the username from the
configured header in every request, no matter where the request came from.
If rest-server was reachable without going through the authenticating proxy,
for example because it listened on all interfaces, anyone could impersonate
any user by setting that header themselves. The username from the header was
also not checked against the rules that apply to usernames in `.htpasswd`.

Rest-server now refuses to start with `--proxy-auth-username` when it listens
on a non-loopback address, unless the new `--trusted-proxies` option lists the
IP addresses or CIDR prefixes of the proxies. When `--trusted-proxies` is set,
requests from any other address are rejected, and with `--proxy-headers` the
forwarded headers of such requests are ignored. Usernames from the header must
now consist of letters, digits and the characters `@`, `.`, `_` and `-`, like
usernames in `.htpasswd`; other usernames are rejected.

Setups that listen on a loopback address or a unix socket are not affected.
Setups that listen on another address, for example inside a container, must
add `--trusted-proxies` with the address of their proxy.

https://github.com/restic/rest-server/issues/413
